**Saved:** `docs/content-analysis/perspective-reviews/Caitlin-Clark/2026-08-05_020002/critic.md`

**Verdict:** trust `strained` · value `useful` · delight `clear_hit` · recommendation **revise** · **5 blockers, 9 concerns**

**Strongest hit** — the Swoopes concession (H2 6). The article grants a hostile critic on the merits *and* specifies the merit: "The take was mocked into the ground, and it was partly correct… Clark's rookie efficiency was ordinary by superstar standards… on the timeline she was right." It concedes before it needs goodwill, not after. Runner-up: "She says the credit belongs to the Black women who built the place. She also keeps the check."

**Highest-priority concern** — the article's own decision procedure is compromised at every point it names. The pre-write memo designates three tiebreakers for Type 1 over Type 3; in the frozen draft all three are damaged in the same direction: the ESPN quote is compressed to remove an outcome goal, the practice-technical scene is unsourced in its specifics and converts a recurring pattern into one softened incident, and the Olympic paragraph omits the half of the TIME interview where Clark maintained she was good enough for the roster. The single sharpest instance is CRITIC-B4: the "what would change our mind" list in the counterargument section cites an interior monologue the article invented four sections earlier — the piece quoting itself as disconfirming evidence.

Research turned up two things the packet didn't have: Carrington's criticism ran the same day as Clark's June 2024 statement, so "Twelve days later, she said this" presents a pressured statement as a spontaneous one; and Monica McNutt's "accountability and self awareness" charge was generated by the exact July 2026 interview the draft quotes three times for Clark's grievance, and appears nowhere.
